WEBVTT

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:02.646
Last time, a hundred grams of water and

00:00:02.707 --> 00:00:04.952
ten grams of salt made a hundred and

00:00:05.052 --> 00:00:07.979
ten grams of solution, because every particle was

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:08.461
still there.

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:10.828
Dissolving is a physical change.

00:00:11.652 --> 00:00:13.618
So what happens to mass in a chemical

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.080
reaction?

00:00:14.944 --> 00:00:17.252
Here's green copper carbonate in a crucible.

00:00:17.797 --> 00:00:19.350
Heat it, and it turns black.

00:00:19.916 --> 00:00:22.607
And the balance reading falls, by zero point

00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:23.772
eight eight of a gram.

00:00:24.476 --> 00:00:26.144
Did some mass just get destroyed?

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:27.174
No.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:30.446
Heating breaks the copper carbonate down into two

00:00:30.586 --> 00:00:34.174
new substances: black copper oxide, and carbon dioxide

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:34.534
gas.

00:00:35.357 --> 00:00:37.406
The copper oxide stays in the crucible, but

00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:39.135
the gas drifts out into the air.

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.008
Draw a line around what's on the balance.

00:00:42.491 --> 00:00:44.980
We'll call everything inside that line the system.

00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:48.056
The carbon dioxide crossed that line, so the

00:00:48.118 --> 00:00:49.363
balance stopped weighing it.

00:00:50.027 --> 00:00:51.851
It has a mass of zero point eight

00:00:52.053 --> 00:00:54.318
eight of a gram, exactly the mass that

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:54.779
vanished.

00:00:55.603 --> 00:00:56.529
Now a quick prediction.

00:00:57.132 --> 00:00:59.059
Burn a strip of magnesium ribbon in a

00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:01.387
crucible, and it flares into white powder.

00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:04.012
Will the mass go up, go down, or

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:04.512
stay the same?

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:08.728
It goes up.

00:01:09.515 --> 00:01:11.257
Zero point two four of a gram of

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:14.261
magnesium becomes zero point four zero of a

00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:16.285
gram of white magnesium oxide.

00:01:16.889 --> 00:01:18.396
The extra zero point one six of a

00:01:18.438 --> 00:01:19.704
gram is oxygen.

00:01:20.308 --> 00:01:22.015
It came from the air, crossed into the

00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:23.864
system, and joined the magnesium.

00:01:24.627 --> 00:01:26.610
Burning things often seems to make them lighter,

00:01:26.911 --> 00:01:28.192
like a log turning to ash,

00:01:28.714 --> 00:01:30.777
but that's because the gases it makes float

00:01:30.798 --> 00:01:31.017
away.

00:01:32.018 --> 00:01:33.691
So what if nothing crosses the line?

00:01:34.516 --> 00:01:38.047
Here, two clear solutions, sodium carbonate and calcium

00:01:38.088 --> 00:01:40.214
chloride, sit in beakers on the balance.

00:01:40.937 --> 00:01:42.940
Pour one into the other, and a cloudy

00:01:43.021 --> 00:01:44.043
white solid forms,

00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:45.586
but no gas is made.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:48.574
Nothing crosses the line, so the reading doesn't

00:01:48.615 --> 00:01:48.955
change.

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:51.646
That's the law of conservation of mass.

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:55.013
In a closed system, where nothing crosses, the

00:01:55.074 --> 00:01:57.898
total mass after a reaction equals the total

00:01:57.939 --> 00:01:58.659
mass before.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.451
Why must mass be conserved?

00:02:02.076 --> 00:02:04.203
Because a reaction only rearranges atoms.

00:02:04.806 --> 00:02:07.313
Bonds break and new bonds form, but no

00:02:07.455 --> 00:02:09.901
atom is created or destroyed, and each atom

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.085
keeps its own mass.

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.983
Look at hydrogen burning in oxygen to make

00:02:14.044 --> 00:02:14.305
water.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.761
The same four hydrogen atoms and two oxygen

00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:19.569
atoms are there before and after.

00:02:20.112 --> 00:02:21.317
They're just joined up differently.

00:02:22.043 --> 00:02:23.657
Same atoms, so the same mass.

00:02:24.335 --> 00:02:26.526
So a chemical equation has to count the

00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:28.396
same atoms on each side.

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:30.340
Here's the reaction written quickly:

00:02:31.061 --> 00:02:33.823
H two, plus O two, makes H two

00:02:33.844 --> 00:02:33.883
O.

00:02:35.013 --> 00:02:35.601
Count the oxygen.

00:02:36.168 --> 00:02:37.961
Two on the left, but only one on

00:02:38.002 --> 00:02:38.344
the right.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.172
Read literally, this equation says an oxygen atom

00:02:42.313 --> 00:02:44.314
vanished, and atoms can't do that.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.131
It isn't balanced yet.

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:48.754
There are two kinds of number in an

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:49.258
equation.

00:02:49.882 --> 00:02:52.259
The small number inside a formula is a

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:52.803
subscript.

00:02:53.366 --> 00:02:55.254
It says how many atoms are in one

00:02:55.314 --> 00:02:57.864
particle, so it fixes what the substance is.

00:02:58.587 --> 00:03:00.471
Change H two O to H two O

00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:03.779
two, and you've written hydrogen peroxide, a different

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:04.140
chemical.

00:03:05.003 --> 00:03:06.908
The big number in front of a formula

00:03:07.271 --> 00:03:08.133
is a coefficient.

00:03:08.635 --> 00:03:10.642
It says how many of those particles there

00:03:10.762 --> 00:03:10.863
are.

00:03:11.586 --> 00:03:14.475
To balance an equation, you only change coefficients.

00:03:15.437 --> 00:03:15.967
Let's balance it.

00:03:16.648 --> 00:03:18.649
To get two oxygen atoms on the right,

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.771
we need two water molecules, so write a

00:03:21.812 --> 00:03:23.252
two in front of H two O.

00:03:24.054 --> 00:03:26.387
Now the right has four hydrogen atoms, but

00:03:26.408 --> 00:03:27.353
the left has two.

00:03:27.998 --> 00:03:29.142
So write a two in front of H

00:03:29.544 --> 00:03:30.266
two as well.

00:03:30.931 --> 00:03:31.334
Count again.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.350
Four hydrogen and two oxygen on each side.

00:03:34.993 --> 00:03:37.981
Two hydrogen molecules and one oxygen molecule make

00:03:38.122 --> 00:03:39.124
two water molecules.

00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:41.173
Now magnesium.

00:03:41.837 --> 00:03:45.773
Magnesium plus oxygen makes magnesium oxide, M g

00:03:45.974 --> 00:03:46.074
O.

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:49.566
Oxygen gas is made of O two molecules,

00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:51.431
so there are two oxygen atoms on the

00:03:51.513 --> 00:03:53.618
left, and one on the right.

00:03:54.281 --> 00:03:56.124
Put a two in front of M g

00:03:56.145 --> 00:03:58.268
O, then a two in front of magnesium

00:03:58.349 --> 00:03:58.769
to match.

00:03:59.471 --> 00:04:02.899
Two magnesium plus O two makes two M

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:03.400
g O.

00:04:04.403 --> 00:04:06.846
The letter g in brackets after O two

00:04:06.947 --> 00:04:09.109
marks a gas, and that's the oxygen that

00:04:09.151 --> 00:04:10.271
crossed into the crucible.

00:04:11.274 --> 00:04:12.105
And copper carbonate?

00:04:12.929 --> 00:04:15.954
C u C O three makes C u

00:04:15.975 --> 00:04:17.297
O plus C O two.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:18.259
Count it:

00:04:18.802 --> 00:04:19.282
one copper,

00:04:19.804 --> 00:04:22.308
one carbon and three oxygens on each side.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:23.737
It's already balanced.

00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:26.805
Here, the g marks the carbon dioxide as

00:04:26.846 --> 00:04:28.929
a gas, the one that crossed out of

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:29.330
the system.

00:04:30.535 --> 00:04:33.121
Conservation of mass also lets you calculate mass

00:04:33.242 --> 00:04:33.782
changes.

00:04:34.465 --> 00:04:36.390
The masses of the reactants add up to

00:04:36.411 --> 00:04:37.574
the masses of the products.

00:04:38.257 --> 00:04:40.681
For magnesium, zero point two four of a

00:04:40.722 --> 00:04:43.829
gram, plus the oxygen, equals zero point four

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:45.914
zero of a gram, so the oxygen was

00:04:45.955 --> 00:04:47.557
zero point one six of a gram.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.982
For copper carbonate, two point four seven grams

00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:53.745
makes one point five nine grams of copper

00:04:53.806 --> 00:04:54.125
oxide,

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:56.588
so the carbon dioxide must be zero point

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:57.669
eight eight of a gram.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.699
The equation tells you which substance crossed the

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:01.041
boundary.

00:05:01.444 --> 00:05:03.138
The balance tells you how much.

00:05:03.957 --> 00:05:04.944
Here's a line to remember.

00:05:05.469 --> 00:05:06.012
Atoms in,

00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:07.040
atoms out.

00:05:07.643 --> 00:05:09.026
Change the big number in front,

00:05:09.548 --> 00:05:11.412
never the small number inside.

00:05:14.087 --> 00:05:14.517
Your turn.

00:05:15.382 --> 00:05:18.728
Aluminium reacts with oxygen gas to form aluminium

00:05:18.928 --> 00:05:19.389
oxide,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.553
A l two O three.

00:05:22.338 --> 00:05:23.195
Balance the equation.

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:24.615
Pause here to answer.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.918
Aluminium oxide has three oxygen atoms,

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:40.348
and oxygen gas comes in pairs.

00:05:41.091 --> 00:05:43.435
Six is the smallest number both fit, so

00:05:43.657 --> 00:05:46.542
use three O two and two A l

00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:47.364
two O three.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.853
That's four aluminium atoms on the right, so

00:05:50.954 --> 00:05:52.717
write a four in front of aluminium.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.046
Four A l plus three O two makes

00:05:56.247 --> 00:05:57.810
two A l two O three.

00:05:58.493 --> 00:06:00.640
Writing O three to fix the oxygen is

00:06:00.681 --> 00:06:01.563
the classic slip.

00:06:02.046 --> 00:06:04.083
O three is ozone, a different substance.

00:06:05.843 --> 00:06:06.246
Last one.

00:06:07.031 --> 00:06:09.754
A student heats five point zero zero grams

00:06:09.814 --> 00:06:12.957
of calcium carbonate in an open crucible until

00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:14.438
the mass stops changing.

00:06:15.219 --> 00:06:17.748
Two point eight zero grams of calcium oxide

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:18.390
is left.

00:06:19.292 --> 00:06:22.155
Calculate the mass of carbon dioxide released, and

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:24.278
explain why the mass decreased.

00:06:25.003 --> 00:06:25.955
Pause here to answer.

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:39.912
Five point zero zero minus two point eight

00:06:39.973 --> 00:06:43.056
zero is two point two zero grams of

00:06:43.098 --> 00:06:43.898
carbon dioxide.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.884
The mass of the reactant still equals the

00:06:46.925 --> 00:06:49.350
mass of the products, but carbon dioxide is

00:06:49.391 --> 00:06:52.076
a gas that left the open crucible, so

00:06:52.097 --> 00:06:53.439
the balance no longer weighed it.

00:06:54.161 --> 00:06:56.247
Saying the mass was destroyed is the slip

00:06:56.269 --> 00:06:56.709
to avoid.

00:06:58.847 --> 00:06:59.394
One more thing.

00:07:00.081 --> 00:07:02.491
A balanced equation counts particles.

00:07:03.154 --> 00:07:06.593
Two magnesium atoms react with one oxygen molecule.

00:07:07.255 --> 00:07:08.996
But that zero point two four of a

00:07:09.036 --> 00:07:11.858
gram of magnesium holds an enormous number of

00:07:11.938 --> 00:07:12.218
atoms,

00:07:12.719 --> 00:07:14.280
far too many to count one by one.

00:07:14.941 --> 00:07:17.185
Next time, we'll see how a mole connects

00:07:17.246 --> 00:07:19.952
that particle count with an amount chemists can

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:20.994
actually use.

00:07:23.761 --> 00:07:24.584
Here are the key notes.

00:07:25.107 --> 00:07:26.655
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:26.695 --> 00:07:27.117
to copy them.
